  "three": "Market will eventually settle on fundamental human lives. In the early stage of AI and automation, human lives were often seen as objects - object within themselves and other objects. A groundwork to obsolesce humans was put in motion. Ultimately, humans demand pay and loyalty. On the other hand, computers are upfront on their requirement - the diffusion between object and machine. One might argue that writing codes might challenge the moral property and free will. On the other hand, those technologies will empower humanity. Maybe not in our lifetime but speak about thousands of years ahead. An AI-driven ethical or physically immoral thought will help establish the path of humanity."
